WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:03.500
Film Courage: Can we talk about outlining…do
you prepare an outline before you write?

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:05.130
Blayne Weaver: I’d rather not.

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:08.200
But usually that’s required for the job.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:14.980
Like if I am getting paid for it, they’ll
want to know…these days to get hired to

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:19.730
write a script you pretty much have to write
the script, like you have to write a 35-page

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:26.769
outline for a 90-page script and turn it in
before you can get hired to write the script.

00:00:26.769 --> 00:00:31.630
So when I’m writing for other people, I
do whatever it takes to make them comfortable

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:33.899
with it, which normally involves an outline.

00:00:33.899 --> 00:00:41.410
For me, it’s kind of like…I’m not a
musician but I feel like it’s just jazz,

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:47.870
I feel when it’s time for a change, I can
feel when we need to know more about a character.

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:55.179
It’s very (my way of working) it’s very
artificial to say “At this point on page

00:00:55.179 --> 00:00:58.719
12 we need to learn that Charlie has a secret.”

00:00:58.719 --> 00:00:59.719
You know what I mean?

00:00:59.719 --> 00:01:01.140
Because I don’t know what the secret is?

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.140
Do I make it up now?

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:05.650
Or do I wait until I get to know who Charlie
is?

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:06.830
You know, write that in.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:12.930
And like I feel like the music of it, I just
know on page 12 that maybe there is something

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.400
he’s not sharing with us, maybe it’s this?

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.840
It’s just way more organic and I feel like
it flows.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.380
A lot of times with outlines I’ll find when
I’m writing the actual script I won’t

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:26.860
feel it going the way that the outline was,
you know what I mean?

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:30.850
So it’s either I do something false.

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:35.840
Writing for me (a lot of times), especially
the rewrite process for other’s people’s

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.140
work is taking a round peg and putting it
into a square hole.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:45.720
It’s like here’s a movie…this is an
actual true story, I had written a film that

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:53.060
was a beach, sports, kind of romantic comedy
that was set in Thailand.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.730
The note I got from the studio was “Great.

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:56.730
Love it!

00:01:56.730 --> 00:02:00.740
Only now it’s in Bulgaria and it’s Christmas.”

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:07.750
So my rewrite was to completely and utterly
change the entire scenery and what’s happening

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:08.830
right now?

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:12.430
Round peg, square hole and it’s like a Rubik’s
Cube kind of problem.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:15.410
It doesn’t necessarily lead to good writing.

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:20.140
Like when you’re trying to problem solve
instead of telling the most compelling story,

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:22.920
that’s what I hate about outlining.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:29.840
Like bing pushed into a corner by some random
decision I made three months ago when I was

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.370
just trying to make people feel comfortable
about what the movie was going to be, does

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:34.370
that make sense?

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:37.640
I can ramble about it for the next two hours
because it’s something that I struggle with

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:38.990
a lot.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:42.470
Film Courage: When you finish a script, what’s
your process?

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:46.410
Let’s suppose you’ve gone through several
drafts and it’s for yourself.

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:47.910
This is not writing for someone else.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:49.880
And you’re like “I feel good.

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:51.390
I’ve had a couple people look at it.”

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:52.390
Then what’s your process?

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:57.700
Blayne Weaver: Well, what’s cool about being
able to write the movie and then make it and

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:01.240
stuff which is my favorite of course, is that
it’s never done.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:07.240
We get a whole new process, we get a cast,
I get notes from the actors, I do a read-through

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:12.120
with as much of the cast as I can pull together,
then I get notes.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.910
Then talk about that, then we get on set,
and we’ll talk about it then.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:21.120
What’s great about being in charge is that
I know I’m not Shakespeare.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:27.040
I know that there is always room for improvement
and the key to being a good project manager

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:33.110
or a good director is like being able to listen
to everybody’s suggestions and then make

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:37.340
a clear, distinctive choice and then stick
with that choice, you know what I mean?

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:42.790
Like I can’t tell you how many times I’ve
been really happy with the scene and gotten

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:47.470
on set and the actor has said something funnier
and I’ve just been like “Well, darn!

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:49.230
There you go.

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:53.020
You get the line that’s going to be in the
trailer and I did not write it.”

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:59.349
I mean I had Dave Foley in SIX MONTH RULE
from KIDS IN THE HALL and that guy…I mean

00:03:59.349 --> 00:04:04.209
first of all, the only actor to ever come
up to me and ask if he could do a different

00:04:04.209 --> 00:04:07.550
line reading, like most actors just change
the line.

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:11.710
And I’m certainly not offended by that,
it’s like I’ve done it myself.

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:15.270
But he came up to me and he’s like “Blayne,
I have a line suggestion.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:16.350
Would you like to hear it?”

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:19.709
And I’m like “Dave, yes I would.

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:24.229
And I can go ahead and tell you that you can
probably say whatever you want.”

00:04:24.229 --> 00:04:28.289
And he’s hilarious and he elevated every
single one of his scenes, which were pretty

00:04:28.289 --> 00:04:30.689
funny to begin with.

00:04:30.689 --> 00:04:33.849
And I get to take credit for that because
I’m the director.

00:04:33.849 --> 00:04:37.309
I let him say that, see that’s how smart
I am.

00:04:37.309 --> 00:04:40.389
I cast him and then I let him be him.

